          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy in watt-hours, not milliamp-hours. One 20,000 to 25,000 mAh USB-C bank per household, plus a cheap pocket bank per person, covers most short outages, and the Anker 737 is the one charger we would tell everyone to own.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Once the outage plan includes a router, a CPAP or several people for more than a day, a 300 Wh station is better value per charge. When the fridge joins the list, you are shopping for a 1 kWh power station, and that is a backup decision rather than a charging one.
          </p>
